## 1. Why the morphism must be restricted

An unrestricted quasi-isomorphism remembers cohomology but can forget the
specific data MTT is trying to explain: the admissible stratum, first-order
phase, cyclic normalization, shared-line holonomy, branch type, and the
homotopy that produces higher interactions. The relevant local object is
therefore not just a complex. Write a nested repair germ schematically as

```text
R=(M_adm,A,E,Q,star,tau,g,L_sh,T,V,d_(beta,V)).
```

Here `A` is the internal graded algebra, `E` its graded module, `Q` an odd
superconnection with curvature `Phi=Q^2`, `tau` the cyclic pairing, `g` the
Hilbert metric used for adjoints, `L_sh` the shared differential line, and `T`
the fixed typing data. The scalar repair potential and outer differential are

```text
V=(1/2r) Tr(Phi^* Phi),
d_(beta,V)=exp(-beta V) d_M exp(beta V),
```

where the displayed normalized trace is one admissible finite normalization.
In a continuum model its replacement must be declared explicitly.

## 2. Restricted contraction arrows

For local objects over the same admissible base, a restricted contraction arrow
from `R` to `R_H` consists of

```text
C=(p,i,h; ell; T)
```

and must satisfy the following clauses.

### C0. Type and admissibility

The base stratum, fixed principal symbol where one exists, domains, boundary
conditions, descent representation, and branch label are preserved. A finite
model with no principal symbol must say so rather than treating this clause as
vacuous evidence for a continuum result.

### C1. Normalized contraction

```text
p i = id,
Q h+h Q=id-i p,
h^2=p h=h i=0.
```

For homological transfer, the corresponding contraction identities for the
internal differential `m1` must also hold.

### C2. Curved first-order intertwining

```text
p Q=Q_H p,
Q i=i Q_H,
p Q^*=Q_H^* p.
```

The adjoint clause is separate. It cannot be inferred from a bare
quasi-isomorphism.

### C3. Cyclic and metric normalization

The inclusion and projection are adjoint for the declared cyclic pairing, the
homotopy is cyclic with the appropriate graded sign, the unit is retained, and
the normalization defining `V` agrees on source and target. The Hilbert metric
used by the outer adjoint is also retained.

### C4. Shared-line equivariance

The line map `ell` preserves the connection and holonomy, and `p,i,h,Q` are
equivariant. Integer circle weights may then be reduced to finite characters,
but a finite character alone does not reconstruct the differential line.

### C5. Outer repair compatibility

The contraction is parallel over `M_adm`, the scalar potential pulls back
exactly, and source and target use the same `beta`. Therefore it can be extended
coefficientwise to the outer de Rham/Witten complexes.

### C6. Transfer control

The perturbation trees defining `m_n` must terminate or converge. A finite
nilpotent filtration, an explicit truncation theorem, or an analytic majorant is
required. The contraction data are part of the arrow because different
homotopies can produce different representatives of the transferred higher
operations.

These clauses are summarized as a reusable evidence contract in
`docs/RESTRICTED_NESTED_REPAIR_MORPHISM_CONTRACT.md`.

## 3. Local category theorem

### Theorem 3.1

Restricted contractions with fixed typing data form a local category. The
identity arrow is

```text
(id,id,0).
```

If

```text
C1=(p1,i1,h1): R0 -> R1,
C2=(p2,i2,h2): R1 -> R2,
```

then their composite is

```text
p=p2 p1,
i=i1 i2,
h=h1+i1 h2 p1.
```

### Proof

The intertwining and typing clauses are closed under composition. For the
homotopy identity, use `Q0 i1=i1 Q1` and `p1 Q0=Q1 p1`:

```text
Q0 h+h Q0
 =(id-i1 p1)+i1(id-i2 p2)p1
 =id-i1 i2 p2 p1.
```

The side conditions follow from those of `C1,C2`. For three arrows, both
parenthesizations give

```text
h1+i1 h2 p1+i1 i2 h3 p2 p1,
```

so composition is associative. The cyclic, metric, shared-line, and outer
clauses compose by their ordinary functorial laws. QED.

This is a local category theorem. A stack theorem additionally requires
effective gluing and cocycle descent over overlaps; that is not asserted here.

## 4. Derived-shadow theorem

### Theorem 4.1

Every restricted contraction arrow preserves the following strict shadows:

```text
Q and Q^*,
Phi=Q^2,
the declared normalized V,
d_(beta,V) and d_(beta,V)^*,
both positive Witten-square blocks,
shared-line weights and holonomy,
the retained-sector projector `i p`.
```

It also canonically transfers the internal products `m2,m3,...` determined by
the declared contraction and its convergence clause.

### Proof

Curvature intertwining is forced by the first-order relation:

```text
p Phi=p Q^2=Q_H^2 p=Phi_H p.
```

If `X` is also intertwined, the Jordan and Lie shadows follow by applying this
identity to `Phi X+X Phi` and `Phi X-X Phi`. Clause C3 supplies equality of the
chosen normalized scalar cost. Since `p` is parallel over the base and commutes
with multiplication by `V`,

```text
p d_(beta,V)=d_(beta,V_H) p.
```

The adjoint clause gives the same statement for `d_(beta,V)^*`; multiplication
then gives both Witten squares. Shared-line equivariance is C4. Finally,
homological perturbation expresses every transferred `m_n` as a sum of rooted
trees built from `p,i,h` and the source products. C6 makes those sums legitimate.
QED.

The last statement is transfer, not strict projection. In particular, a source
DGA may have `m3=0` while its reduced model has `m3!=0`. Writing
`p m3=m3_H p^3` in that situation would be false.

## 5. Exact same-source witness

Let

```text
A=Lambda(a,b,c),
d a=d b=0,
d c=a wedge b.
```

Give it circle weights

```text
w(a)=1,
w(b)=1,
w(c)=2.
```

Let `Gamma_A` be parity and let `S=R^(2|2)`. On `S`, define the real symmetric
odd matrix

```text
C(x) = [ 0    A(x)   ],
       [ A(x)^T  0   ],

A(x) = [ 1   x ],
       [-x   1 ].
```

Direct multiplication gives

```text
C(x)^2=(1+x^2) I_4.
```

On `E=A tensor S`, define one configuration-dependent first-order source

```text
Q_A(x)=d tensor I_4+Gamma_A tensor C(x).
```

Because `d^2=0` and `d Gamma_A=-Gamma_A d`, the cross terms cancel and

```text
Q_A(x)^2=(1+x^2) I_32.
```

Thus the inner curvature and normalized repair cost are emitted by the same
first-order object:

```text
Phi(x)=(1+x^2)I,
V(x)=1/2(1+x^2)^2.
```

At `x=0`, `Phi!=0` and `Phi'=0`. The Gram part of `V''` is zero, the
residual-contracted second jet is `2`, and the full Hessian is `2`.

## 6. Dimension-reducing contraction

Use the Heisenberg contraction

```text
H=span(1,a,b,a wedge c,b wedge c,a wedge b wedge c),
h(a wedge b)=c.
```

Tensor `p,i,h` with `I_4`. The source fiber has dimension `8*4=32`; the target
fiber has dimension `6*4=24`. Since `h` anticommutes with parity,

```text
Q_A h+h Q_A=id-i p.
```

The target operator is

```text
Q_H(x)=Gamma_H tensor C(x),
Q_H(x)^2=(1+x^2)I_24.
```

The same projection intertwines `Q`, `Q^*`, `Phi`, and the orthogonal
retained-sector projector. This is a fiber projector, not yet the physical
admissible-tangent projector on configuration space. Normalized trace rather
than raw trace is essential:
both ranks then give the same `V`.

The top-form functional on `A` supplies a cyclic pairing. The verifier checks
that `d` is cyclic, `p` is adjoint to `i`, and

```text
<h x,y>=(-1)^|x| <x,h y>.
```

## 7. Outer Witten repair from the same curvature

On polynomial sections over the configuration line, set `beta=1` and

```text
A_V=partial_x+V'(x),
A_V^*=-partial_x+V'(x).
```

Here `A_V^*` is the formal polynomial adjoint. Analytic domains and boundary
conditions are not asserted by this witness.

The outer differential on zero- and one-forms is

```text
D_V(f_0,f_1 dx)=(0,A_V f_0),
D_V^2=0.
```

Because `p` is constant in `x` and source and target have the same `V`, it
intertwines `A_V`, `A_V^*`, `A_V^*A_V`, and `A_V A_V^*`. This is the requested
finite same-source conjunction:

```text
one Q_A(x)
  -> one Phi(x)
  -> one normalized V(x)
  -> one outer Witten repair,

one cyclic contraction
  -> one lower Q_H(x)
  -> the same Phi and V
  -> the same outer repair blocks.
```

## 8. Interactions and phase

The same `p,i,h` transfer the wedge product to

```text
m3(a,a,b)=a wedge c,
m3(a,b,b)=-b wedge c.
```

They satisfy the arity-four Stasheff identity and strict unity. Every source
map preserves the integer circle weights and therefore their `Z3` reduction.
The curvature is central and proportional to the retained unit, so it transfers
unchanged and contributes one normalized scalar coordinate.

This shows that phase, curvature, repair, and a nontrivial higher interaction
can coexist in one exact projection packet. It does not show that the selected
MTT source has this local algebra.

